+flow calls rather than hashing or comparing anything itself.
+
+## Reset flow
+
+1. **Request.** `request_reset(email)` generates a token with `secrets` and
+   records it for the account together with the time it was issued. An
+   account can have more than one outstanding token at once (for example, if
+   the user asks for a new email after losing the first one).
+2. **Complete.** `complete_reset(email, token, new_password)` verifies the
+   token against the issued ones for that account using a constant-time
+   comparison (`accounts.crypto.tokens_match`), and only accepts it if it:
+   - matches a token that was actually issued for that account,
+   - was issued within the last 30 minutes, and
+   - has not already been used.
+
+   On success, the new password is stored via `accounts.crypto.hash_password`
+   and **every** outstanding reset token for the account is cleared -- the
+   one just used and any others -- so a token can never be replayed and a
+   stale token from an earlier request stops working once a reset completes.
+3. **Failure.** `complete_reset` returns `False` for an unknown, expired, or
+   already-used token rather than raising, since callers see all three as
+   the same "invalid token" outcome.
+
+`check_token(email, token)` performs the same match-and-expiry check as step
+2 without consuming the token or changing any state, for callers that just
+want to know whether a token is currently valid.
